{
 "id": "2025-lobato-telepsychology",
 "name": "Telepsychology Spaces: Background, Decoration and Therapist Choices",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: potential users of online psychological therapy answer background questions (age, gender, studies, previous therapy, interest in therapy, device) and then make seven quick visual choices in the paper's order: preferred therapist sex; preferred colour (the nine counselling-room colours of Table 1 as swatches); one of three video-call backgrounds rising in complexity and naturalness (Figure 3); dressed-up vs casual female and male therapist figures (Figure 1); high vs low naturalness decoration (green wall vs potted plant) and high vs low complexity decoration (full bookshelf vs small shelf) (Figure 2). The page records the time to each choice. Questions 3–7 use the paper's printed wording. Analysis as in the paper: choice shares by age group, gender, education and therapy experience (chi-square), response time by option (ANOVA on responses under 20 s), decision trees or logistic regression per choice. Original result (n = 310, Spain): medium background chosen most and fastest; high naturalness and complexity decorations preferred; cerulean the favourite colour (38.7%). Differences from the original study: (1) the wording of questions 1 and 2 and of the \"interest in therapy\" and \"device\" questions is mine (the paper gives only their topics and answer levels); (2) image options appear in random left-to-right order, which the paper does not state; (3) colours are shown as swatches without names; (4) no access code or Bizum payment; (5) images are taken from the paper's PDF at print resolution.",
